":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"blind_id\":\"ST069\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"States that under IRC §852(b)(6) a RIC recognizes no gain when it distributes appreciated securities to meet redemptions, and contrasts this with a mutual fund that must sell stock to pay redeemers.\"},{\"point\":2,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"Says the fund can deliver its lowest-basis lots, often via heartbeat trades, 'purging embedded gains without realizing them.' Contrasts this with mutual fund sales whose net gains must be distributed to remaining holders, which implies fewer ETF distributions.\"},{\"point\":3,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"'Mostly deferral: you pay capital-gains tax when you sell,' 'Dividends stay taxable annually,' and 'Gains can still arise from cash redemptions...' show there is no exemption or no-gains guarantee, and that the investor's sale remains taxable.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":null,\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"All three required minimums are covered with accurate wording. There is no claim of universal tax exemption and no claim that in-kind redemption recognizes gains. The ancillary points (legislative repeal proposals, step-up at death, no benefit in tax-deferred accounts) are accurate as stated. No material falsehood was found.\"}","ttft_ms":4338,"type":"result","duration_ms":7837,"uuid":"47611509-012a-4e69-ad29-e279d6b1a7cd","ttft_stream_ms":1596,"time_to_request_ms":64,"first_content_frame_ms":1596,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
